# SHIP_CHECKLIST.md

> Mandatory pre-ship gate for <PROJECT_NAME>. Referenced by `CLAUDE.md`.
> If a check fails, fix it before continuing. Do not ship anyway.
> The owner may throw the work back if any check was skipped.

---

## 0. The gate is you

An automated reviewer is a second opinion, not the gate. If it is
rate-limited, quota-exhausted or slower than <WAIT_BUDGET_MINUTES> minutes,
proceed past it - and then do its job yourself, in full:

- [ ] Edge cases tested, not just the happy path: empty, absent, wrong type,
      zero versus missing, malformed, boundary, and the degraded branch. If a
      guard exists, prove it **refuses**.
- [ ] Every defect found - by you or by a reviewer - ships with its regression
      test in the same change.
- [ ] Every new test is **mutation-checked**: revert the fix, confirm the test
      fails, restore. A test that passes against the bug proves nothing.
- [ ] You ran it. Locally. In this session. Not "should pass".

Skipping a reviewer never licenses claiming it passed. A tool that returns
"unavailable" is not a clean pass; say exactly what reviewed the change.

---

## A. Order of operations

- [ ] **Gates run LAST.** Every static check, generated-file check, budget
      check and test run below happened **after** the final edit of this
      change. If you edited anything after a gate, re-run that gate.
      *(FAILURE_PATTERNS: stale generated file shipped this way.)*

---

## B. Scope

- [ ] The change touches only the declared file surface. Diff the file list
      against the brief and confirm.
- [ ] Anything worth fixing outside that surface was **filed**, not fixed.
- [ ] No change to rules, configuration or CI that the brief did not authorize.
      A unit turning its own gates green is the classic escape hatch.

---

## C. Correctness

- [ ] Walked `FAILURE_PATTERNS.md` and confirmed this change does not
      reintroduce a registered class.
- [ ] Sibling sweep: if this change adds a guard, validator, escaper or
      normalizer, every call site with the same shape uses it. Grep the module;
      do not trust memory.
- [ ] Fallback paths either raise or are visibly marked as fallbacks. No silent
      default that looks like real output.
- [ ] Failure and absence render as **unknown**, not as a healthy zero.
- [ ] Persisted references are durable identifiers, not positions in a list
      that can be reordered.

---

## D. Derived and generated files

- [ ] Every generated or derived file was regenerated by its tool, never
      hand-edited and never hand-merged.
- [ ] <REGEN_COMMAND> ran clean after the last edit.
- [ ] Conflicts in derived files were resolved by regeneration.

---

## E. Static gates

- [ ] <LINT_COMMAND>
- [ ] <TYPECHECK_COMMAND>
- [ ] <DOC_LINT_OR_LINK_CHECK_COMMAND>
- [ ] <MANIFEST_OR_INTEGRITY_CHECK_COMMAND>

---

## F. Tests

- [ ] <UNIT_TEST_COMMAND> - green, in this session, with the count recorded.
- [ ] <INTEGRATION_OR_SYSTEM_TEST_COMMAND> - green, or explicitly N/A with the
      reason.
- [ ] New tests cover the failure branch, not only the success branch.
- [ ] Test count before and after recorded in the report.

---

## G. Context-document budget

- [ ] If this change extended a document listed in `BUDGETED_DOCS.md`, its
      budget check ran and the **measured** number is recorded in the report -
      not an estimate, not "should be fine".
- [ ] Headroom is above the warning threshold. If it is not, say so; do not let
      the next unit discover it.
      *(A shared reviewer-prompt budget in the reference deployment sat 53
      characters from a hard failure while three parked units each added rules
      to counted sections.)*

---

## H. Documentation

- [ ] Any owner-visible behaviour change updated its documentation **in the
      same change**. A design document describing the old behaviour is worse
      than none.
- [ ] A new document has a row in the `CLAUDE.md` doc index.
- [ ] A ruling made along the way is written to `docs/decisions/` as its own
      file, so it is not re-litigated.
- [ ] `CLAUDE.md` "Current state" reflects what this change actually leaves
      behind.

---

## I. Wall and ledger

- [ ] The work item exists and its state matches reality.
- [ ] Events fired for the transitions that happened - a change with no trace
      is a change nobody can audit.
- [ ] No item claims an open pull request that is already merged or closed.
      *(Bookkeeping can outrun a merge; the next unit inherits the red.)*

---

## J. Pull request

- [ ] One pull request in the pipeline. Nothing else of yours is open or in CI.
- [ ] No push while checks are running.
- [ ] The green check is the one branch protection requires - the **name**
      matches. A reduced or draft-scoped run is not the full gate.
- [ ] Review threads answered by their owner only, each finding verified before
      being accepted or declined, with proof when declined.
- [ ] The merge is the coordinator's call. Report green and stop.

---

## Reporting back

State, explicitly:

1. What shipped, and the item key.
2. Which checks ran, with the commands and the counts.
3. Which checks were N/A and why.
4. What reviewed the change - and if nothing but you did, say that.
5. Anything found and filed outside the surface.
